{
  "dom_id": "step-q351442",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=1.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(t)){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
